{
  "id": "charles__law",
  "title": "Charles' Law",
  "description": "Calculate the volume of an ideal gas vs. temperature using Charles' Law. In a perfect gas where mass and pressure are constant, volume varies directly with absolute temperature.",
  "type": "datatable",
  "components": [
    {
      "type": "datatable",
      "headers": [
        "State",
        "Volume (L)",
        "Temperature (K)"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Initial (State 1)",
          2,
          273
        ],
        [
          "Final (State 2)",
          2.73,
          373
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "content": "## Charles' Law Formula\n\n**V₁ / T₁ = V₂ / T₂**\n\nWhere:\n- V = volume (m³, ft³, L, etc.)\n- T = absolute temperature (K, °R)\n\n## Example Calculation\nA gas occupies a volume of 2 liters at 0°C (273 K). The volume at 100°C (373 K):\n\nV₂ = (T₂ × V₁) / T₁ = (373 K × 2 L) / (273 K) = 2.73 L"
    },
    {
      "type": "calculator",
      "inputs": [
        {
          "id": "V1",
          "label": "Initial Volume (V₁)",
          "type": "number",
          "defaultValue": 2,
          "unit": "L"
        },
        {
          "id": "T1",
          "label": "Initial Temperature (T₁)",
          "type": "number",
          "defaultValue": 273,
          "unit": "K"
        },
        {
          "id": "T2",
          "label": "Final Temperature (T₂)",
          "type": "number",
          "defaultValue": 373,
          "unit": "K"
        }
      ],
      "outputs": [
        {
          "id": "V2",
          "label": "Final Volume (V₂)",
          "formula": "(inputs.T2 * inputs.V1) / inputs.T1",
          "unit": "L",
          "format": "decimal"
        },
        {
          "id": "ratio",
          "label": "Volume Ratio (V₂/V₁)",
          "formula": "inputs.T2 / inputs.T1",
          "format": "decimal"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}